---
import SeoPage from '../../layouts/SeoPage.astro';

const LEARN = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftteams/meeting-transcription-captions';
const REC = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftteams/cloud-recording';
const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Does the bot need a Microsoft account?',
    a: 'No. It joins Teams meetings in the browser as a guest and is never signed in to Microsoft. Calendar auto-join for Outlook needs your own Azure app registration, but pasting a meeting link needs none.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What stops it from joining?',
    a: 'The organiser\'s tenant decides. If anonymous guests are blocked, or a lobby admits only people from the organisation, the bot cannot join without help. OpenMinutes shows the reason on the meeting page and does not work around it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is this different from Teams\' own transcription?',
    a: 'Teams\' built-in transcription is controlled by policy for the organiser and the person starting it, and stores the transcript with the recording in OneDrive or SharePoint. OpenMinutes is a separate guest that records audio and keeps the transcript in your own database.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will participants know?',
    a: 'Yes. The bot is visible in the participant list under its own name and can post a message in the chat. You are responsible for the consent rules that apply.',
  },
];
---

<SeoPage
  title="Self-hosted Microsoft Teams meeting transcription | OpenMinutes"
  description="OpenMinutes joins Microsoft Teams meetings as a browser guest and writes a transcript with speaker names on your own server. What works and what tenants can block."
  h1="Self-hosted Microsoft Teams meeting transcription"
  lede="OpenMinutes sends a bot into your Teams meetings as a guest, records the audio and gives you a transcript with names on a server you run."
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Use cases', path: '/compare' }, { name: 'Teams transcription', path: '/use/microsoft-teams-transcription-self-hosted' }]}
  {faq}
  related={[
    { href: '/docs/teams', label: 'Teams setup guide' },
    { href: '/use/google-meet-notes-self-hosted', label: 'Google Meet notes, self-hosted' },
    { href: '/use/gdpr-meeting-notes', label: 'Meeting notes and GDPR' },
    { href: '/compare', label: 'Compare with hosted notetakers' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="callout callout-warning">
    <p>
      <strong>Young support</strong>
      Teams support is new. Tests run against fake Teams pages, not live recordings, and Teams changes its web app without notice.
      If joining or speaker names break, open an issue with the date. OpenMinutes itself is an early release.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>How the bot joins</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Opens the link and chooses to continue in the browser.</li>
    <li>Types its name, mutes the microphone, keeps the camera off unless you set an image, and joins.</li>
    <li>Waits in the lobby until someone admits it, up to a timeout you set.</li>
    <li>Records audio and logs participants and the active speaker, and posts the join message.</li>
    <li>Leaves when the meeting ends, when it is alone for a set time, or at a maximum length.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Links in the usual Teams formats work. See <a href="/docs/teams">the Teams guide</a> for the list.</p>

  <h2>What the organiser's tenant can block</h2>
  <p>
    Joining as a guest only works if the organiser's tenant allows anonymous join. Meetings that require a verified attendee are
    blocked the same way. For another organisation only its admins can change it. If you administer Teams,
    <a href="/docs/teams">the guide</a> shows the two settings.
  </p>

  <h2>Built-in Teams transcription, for context</h2>
  <p>
    Microsoft documents that for a meeting to include a transcript, the organiser must have transcription turned on, and the person
    starting it needs it too (<a href={LEARN} rel="nofollow noopener">Microsoft Learn</a>). Recording works similarly, and admins can
    require explicit consent from participants (<a href={REC} rel="nofollow noopener">Microsoft Learn</a>). Those transcripts live with the recordings in OneDrive or SharePoint.
  </p>
  <p>
    Microsoft also documents a separate route for calling and meeting bots through Microsoft Graph, which needs admin consent in the
    tenant (<a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftteams/platform/bots/calls-and-meetings/registering-calling-bot" rel="nofollow noopener">Microsoft Learn</a>).
    OpenMinutes does not use it. It joins as a browser guest.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the data goes</h2>
  <p>
    Your instance stores audio, transcripts and chat. Transcription runs locally or at a provider you choose with your own key.
    See <a href="/use/self-hosted-ai-meeting-notetaker">self-hosted notetaker</a> and <a href="/use/gdpr-meeting-notes">GDPR notes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to choose it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You cannot ask organisers to allow anonymous guests, and your meetings are in tenants you do not control.</li>
    <li>You need a bot-free or compliance-recording route approved by your Teams admins. OpenMinutes is a visible guest.</li>
    <li>You want a managed, polished product with integrations rather than software you run. See <a href="/compare">the comparisons</a>.</li>
    <li>You use Zoom as well. It is not supported.</li>
  </ul>
</SeoPage>
